window.docContents[83].resolve({"/Metrological-modularity/#metrological-modularity":{"contents":"The chapters so far give the calculus's vocabulary (kinds, quantities, units, dimensions,\nobjects and their parts) and its evidence discipline (the boundary audit and the\nmetrological provenance hypergraph); the authoring recipe and the uncertainty\ncalculus follow. This chapter states the\norganizing principle they add up to: software organized as metrology modules. Nothing\nin the principle is specific to science software — it applies wherever a computed value\nis presented as a measurement result in the VIM sense, whatever the domain; science\nsoftware is simply where this development exercises it. A\nmetrology module is a software component — the\ndeclarations one boundary names as its members, which need not be a file or a\nnamespace — whose\n\n1. interface is a declared boundary of kind-typed\n   ports — input quantities, output quantities, parameters, configuration, and conditional\n   outputs with their deciders;2. behavior is a measurement model in the VIM sense — VIM 4 2CD §2.12 [VIM3: 2.48],\n   the mathematical relation among the quantities involved, in the general implicit form\n    — attached to the boundary as a checked theorem edge,\n   not as prose;3. implementation is a measurement function (VIM 4 2CD §2.13 [VIM3: 2.49], whose\n   Note allows that \"f\" may symbolize an algorithm): a carrier-parametric kinded\n   definition whose relation to the measurement model is equals, inverts, refines,\n   or boundedBy a declared tolerance;4. licenses are stated per rung of the carrier ladder, as the relation's\n   license clause: the measurement-model claim is proved at one carrier, and each\n   further carrier the module runs at — binary32, Float, the tape — gets its own entry\n   saying how the claim holds there. A law proved over the reals survives the move up to\n   one rounding per operation; a side condition phrased in the carrier's own arithmetic,\n   such as \"the total is not zero\", constrains the exact total at one rung and the\n   rounded total at the next, and neither implies the other;5. mereology is declared — each output port carries its aggregation class, and the\n   license to distribute the module's computation over a carving is derived from that\n   declaration, not assumed;6. requirements are stated — what the module's quantities must satisfy, each a\n   requirement beside the boundary it governs with the evidence by which it is\n   met: a provable requirement by a theorem, an empirical requirement by\n   a gate against a referent the model does not define.\n\nThe name pairs the discipline (metrology — the interface vocabulary is Dybkær/VIM kinds\nand quantities, the spec vocabulary is the VIM measurement model) with the software\nprinciple (modularity — boundaries, contracts, composition). Why the relational form\nmatters: VIM's general form is implicit, and its §2.12 Note 1 has the measurand's value\ninferred from the relation. A retrieval module's honest specification is therefore\n\"the output satisfies the forward model's equation\" — exactly, or within a declared\ntolerance — not \"the output is what this loop computes\". The paradigm demotes the loop\nto evidence for the relation, with the relation as the published behavior.\nMODULARITY.md in the repository root is the working plan for carrying the paradigm\ninto the downstream model and deployment tiers.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty","header":"17. Metrological modularity","id":"/Metrological-modularity/#metrological-modularity"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--Synopsis--Verifiable-properties":{"contents":"* assemble of the two bodies' masses gives the pair's mass as an IndividualQuantity about the pair as a whole (type-checked) HCP.L2_ThePair#pair_mass.* Adding the pair's mass to body A's mass fails with a type mismatch, because one is about the whole and the other about a part; adding pair P's mass to pair Q's mass fails the same way (refused) HCP.L2_ThePair#refused_whole_part.* assemble of angular frequency over the pair's bodies fails to type-check: there is no Assembles pairS instance for angular frequency (refused) HCP.L2_ThePair#refused_frequency_assembly.* The normal-mode frequency ω₊ is an angular-frequency kind dedicated to the sort pairS, and it is provably different from any angular-frequency kind dedicated to a body or a spring (type-checked) HCP.L2_ThePair#normal_mode.* assemble of stiffness over springs in parallel type-checks; over springs in series it fails: there is no Assembles seriesS springConstantK instance (refused) HCP.L2_ThePair#parallel_stiffness, HCP.L2_ThePair#refused_series_stiffness.* The parallel assembly equals the measured stiffness of the parallel combination, and the series law 1/k = 1/k₁ + 1/k₂ gives k = 2 for springs of stiffness 6 and 3 (theorem) HCP.L2_ThePair#parallelLaw, HCP.L2_ThePair#licenseCashed, HCP.L2_ThePair#seriesLaw.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L2 — The pair is a system too\u0009Synopsis","header":"5.3.1.3. Verifiable properties","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--Synopsis--Verifiable-properties"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Synopsis--Verifiable-properties":{"contents":"* R1: for every pair, the pair's mass equals the assembly of its two bodies' masses, proved by a theorem (theorem) HCP.L6_Requirements#r1_pair_mass.* R2: there is no Assembles pairS normalModeK instance, so a pair's normal-mode frequency cannot be assembled from its bodies (theorem) HCP.L6_Requirements#r2_no_aggregation.* R3: in every state of pair P, the coupling spring's extension lies within its rated range, decided by the gate withinRatedRange (gate) HCP.L6_Requirements#r3_empirical.* #kind_requirement rejects with an error a requirement scoped to a bare real number, or to a declaration that is not a SortOfSystem (refused) HCP.L6_Requirements#invalid_scopes.* The pinned census lists three governed output ports and none ungoverned or unchecked, and #kind_requirement_clean passes (census) HCP.L6_Requirements#requirement_census.* A requirement scoped to pair P, on an accessor that returns a value for every pair, leaves the row for pair Q marked ⚠ UNGOVERNED (census) HCP.L6_Requirements#scope_gap.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L6 — What must hold\u0009Synopsis","header":"5.7.1.3. Verifiable properties","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Synopsis--Verifiable-properties"}});